    bio: 'Wang Chong was a Chinese philosopher of the Eastern Han dynasty, born in Shangyu in what is now Zhejiang province. He came from a modest family and spent much of his life in relative obscurity and poverty, having served briefly in minor official positions before withdrawing from public life to devote himself to writing. His masterwork, the Lunheng — usually translated as "Balanced Inquiries" or "Discourses Weighed in the Balance" — runs to over 200,000 characters and represents one of the most sustained exercises in critical rationalism in the ancient world.\n\nThe Lunheng is remarkable for its relentlessly skeptical approach to received opinion, popular belief, and official tradition. Wang Chong subjected to critical scrutiny everything from the claim that comets presage disaster to the belief that the legendary sage-kings possessed miraculous powers. He argued for a naturalistic universe governed by consistent patterns — what he called li, principle — rather than by the will of spirits, demons, or the moral reactions of Heaven. For Wang Chong, Heaven does not reward virtue or punish vice: it operates according to its own impersonal nature, indifferent to human affairs. This position placed him at direct odds with the dominant Confucian cosmology of his time, which held that natural disasters were signs of Heaven\'s displeasure with human governance.\n\nPerhaps most strikingly, Wang Chong developed an early and sophisticated theory of knowledge that emphasized the importance of empirical evidence over textual authority. He was deeply suspicious of the tendency to accept claims merely because they were old or appeared in canonical texts — a tendency he saw as widespread among the scholars of his day. He insisted that truth must be tested against experience and that the reputation of a source is no substitute for evidence. This commitment to what we might now call evidential reasoning, combined with his naturalistic cosmology and his willingness to challenge even Confucius when the evidence seemed to demand it, makes Wang Chong a uniquely bracing figure in the history of Chinese philosophy.',
